AI AGENTS
│
├── 1. DEFINITION & FOUNDATIONS
│   ├── What an agent is: a system that receives information, processes it, decides and acts
│   ├── It is not just an LLM (like ChatGPT)
│   └── Combination of: LLM + tools + memory + objective
│
├── 2. WHAT ARE THEY FOR?
│   ├── Complex multi-step tasks (days/weeks/years)
│   ├── Workflow automation
│   └── They outperform the "simple prompt" used by 85% of people
│
├── 3. LEVELS OF AUTONOMY
│   ├── Copilots/assistants → you decide
│   ├── Semi-autonomous (with reasoning) → use tools (searches, downloads)
│   └── Fully autonomous → "do it however you want"
│
├── 4. HOW DO THEY "THINK"?
│   ├── They perform fast operations to predict the next word/operation
│   ├── Context engineering → more and better context = better decisions
│   └── Task decomposition → break the complex into simple
│
├── 5. KEY COMPONENTS OF THE AGENT
│   ├── MEMORY
│   │   ├── Short-term (current flow, like RAM)
│   │   └── Long-term (hard drive: files, data, images)
│   ├── GUARDRAILS
│   │   ├── Limit behavior (e.g., only create/modify/delete certain files)
│   │   └── Security first
│   ├── REFLECTION
│   │   ├── Self-evaluation and self-correction
│   │   └── Two agents dialogue (argument/counterargument)
│   └── TOOLS
│       ├── Without tools: text only (very limited)
│       └── With tools: internet, APIs, prices, calendar, email… (MCP servers)
│
├── 6. LIMIT OF THE SINGLE AGENT → MULTI-AGENT SOLUTION
│   └── A single agent doesn't scale → ecosystems/societies of agents (agentic AI)
│
├── 7. MULTI-AGENT SYSTEMS
│   ├── ROLES
│   │   ├── Planner → splits the big task and assigns it
│   │   ├── Executor → executes (each with its own tools)
│   │   └── Reviewer → reviews, gives feedback, enables self-correction
│   ├── COMMUNICATION
│   │   ├── Messages between agents
│   │   └── Risk: infinite loops → economic cost (paid APIs)
│   └── FLOW DESIGN (pipelines)
│       ├── Sequential (pipeline) → when a step depends on the previous one
│       └── Parallel → independent tasks that are later aggregated
│
├── 8. DESIGN BEST PRACTICES
│   ├── One agent = one task; very clear roles
│   ├── If it plans, it doesn't execute; if it reviews, it doesn't plan
│   ├── Simple communication and structured prompts
│   └── Structured JSON outputs (agents love them)
│
├── 9. REAL PRODUCTION EXAMPLE (Stock-Ops Project)
│   ├── Architecture: FastAPI + PostgreSQL + Docker
│   ├── Orchestrator Agent (Support) → interacts with user and manages inventory DB:
│   │   ├── Tool: list_catalog → lists all products with SKU, price, and stock
│   │   ├── Tool: get_product_by_sku → retrieves details for a specific SKU
│   │   └── Tool: reserve_stock → deducts stock from the database
│   ├── Researcher Agent (Delegated) → performs web searches via DuckDuckGo (DDGS)
│   ├── Local LLM (Qwen 3-4B) running via LM Studio
│   └── Guardrails: strict prompts (no invented prices), Pydantic schemas for data validation
│
└── 10. TIPS BEFORE PRODUCTION
    ├── PERFORMANCE
    │   ├── Latency (fast responses)
    │   └── Cost (concise prompts, minimal responses)
    ├── OBSERVABILITY
    │   ├── Logs, tracing
    │   └── Know what failed, how long it took, which tool it used
    └── SECURITY (critical)
        ├── Control and validation of accesses/permissions
        ├── Prompt injection (hidden scraped text)
        └── Confirmation keywords ("autonomous madness")
